SETUP
First I had to get all my virtual machines installed and ready before I could use them to practice. I set easily recognizable names for each VM so I could tell them apart if I'm connecting from one to the other. I then put them all on 1 virtual network, with the Server VM having a second NIC for NAT with the host, as well. Finally, I also set up VMWare Tools on each machine, so I can copy & paste between my host and the VM, for example.
After installing all of the OSs, I had to set up the Server VM with Active Directory, DHCP, and DNS so I can add all of the computers to a domain. Once the features are installed, I need to promote the server to domain controller.
While promoting to domain controller, I ran into an issue because the Admin password was weak. "net user Administrator [password]" fixes that. Once that was complete, I went into Active Directory Users & Computers and set up a few accounts, just so I can have something to play around with later.
I also got a warning that the server didn't have a static IP, so I gave it an easy to remember (for me) IP and subnet mask. I also pointed the gateway/preferred DNS to itself. For DHCP configuration, I pointed it to my admin credentials (which I set up a new user for, rather than the default Admin account). Next, I set up a new scope and gave it an easy range, set the router IP to the static IP for the server, and activated.
Finally, I needed Routing and Direct Access so the server can properly route traffic. I opened the Routing and Remote Access tool menu, selected NAT, and then pointed it to the externally-facing NIC. The internal NIC is strictly to connect with the client VMs, the external one controls access to the outside world.
I then added everything to the AD domain. In Windows, I have to go into the Network Settings and set the DNS server to the IP of the Server machine. From there, I go to Account settings, Access work or school, join to a local AD domain, and adding it to the domain. I had to input credentials of a domain admin to allow it on the domain.
Kali was next to add to the domain, and it requires more configuration to do so. First, I had to update/upgrade the system and set the DNS to the domain controller again. Then, I had to install several packages to allow me to join Kali to the domain. For simplicity, here is a command that does all of them:
sudo apt -y install realmd libnss-sss libpam-sss sssd sssd-tools adcli samba-common-bin oddjob oddjob-mkhomedir packagekit
From there, I have to make sure the AD domain is discoverable on Kali:
sudo realm discover [domain]
And finally:
sudo realm join --user=[domain admin account] [domain]
Ubuntu will largely follow a similar process, and this will be used for our Wazuh server.

